WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:11.286
"A lie can travel halfway around the world while the truth is still putting on its shoes" is just one of many notable quotations from the writings of Samuel Langhorne Clemens.

2
00:00:11.287 --> 00:00:22.947
More commonly known as Mark Twain, the famous American writer and humorist is best remembered for the novels The Adventures of Tom Sawyer and Adventures of Huckleberry Finn

3
00:00:22.948 --> 00:00:26.897
. His writing is marked by a unique style of humor and

4
00:00:26.898 --> 00:00:33.212
satire. It is also significant for the way it critiqued the social, moral, and political aspects of society.

5
00:00:33.213 --> 00:00:40.053
Samuel Clemens was a very colorful character who became the most popular American celebrity of his time.

6
00:00:40.054 --> 00:00:47.664
Samuel Langhorne Clemens was born on November 30, 1835, in a small village named Florida, Missouri.

7
00:00:47.665 --> 00:00:51.818
At the time of his birth, Halley’s Comet was visible in the sky.

8
00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:57.976
His parents were John and Jane Clemens, and Samuel was the sixth of seven children in his family.

9
00:00:57.977 --> 00:01:03.982
John Clemens was a lawyer who had gained considerable wealth as a land owner in Tennessee.

10
00:01:03.983 --> 00:01:16.193
Due to bad business decisions and unfortunate events, however, the family fortune was lost, and when Samuel Clemens was still a baby, the family moved to the river town of Hannibal, Missouri.

11
00:01:16.194 --> 00:01:25.448
It was this town and his experiences there that inspired his most famous books, particularly The Adventures of Tom Sawyer

12
00:01:25.449 --> 00:01:31.108
. Samuel’s father died in 1847 and left the family with a lot of

13
00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:37.146
debt. It was at this time that the creative interests and talents of Mark Twain began to emerge.

14
00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:44.843
At the age of eleven, after having completed the fifth grade, Samuel Clemens went to work at his brother’s newspaper.

15
00:01:44.844 --> 00:01:48.213
He worked as a printer’s apprentice and even did some writing.

16
00:01:48.214 --> 00:01:55.913
Some of the most controversial stories in the paper came from the mind of Clemens—usually when his brother was out of town.

17
00:01:55.914 --> 00:02:01.870
Clemens continued to earn a living working for newspaper companies in St. Louis and New York City.

18
00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:09.401
Yet in 1857, his love of the Mississippi River drew him back, and he decided to become a steamboat captain.

19
00:02:09.402 --> 00:02:15.644
He thoroughly enjoyed this profession and described his experiences in his book Life on the Mississippi

20
00:02:15.645 --> 00:02:22.803
. Unfortunately, the Civil War put an end to all boating on the river, and thus, ended Clemens’ career aboard

21
00:02:22.804 --> 00:02:29.174
steamboats. However, it was his experiences on the river that gave him his pen name and inspired his future

22
00:02:29.175 --> 00:02:36.166
works. After leaving his beloved Mississippi River, Samuel Clemens volunteered as a soldier in the Confederate

23
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:38.829
army. His time as a soldier, however, was brief.

24
00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:45.781
He quit after just two weeks and was soon on his way to Virginia City, Nevada, to work with his brother again.

25
00:02:45.782 --> 00:02:59.445
There, he wrote for the Territorial Enterprise , a local newspaper, and on February 3, 1863, he signed a humorous travel story with the pen name "Mark Twain" for the first

26
00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:03.871
time. The imaginative name comes from the language of steamboat

27
00:03:03.872 --> 00:03:10.322
sailors. The Mississippi River is shallow and the depth of the water was measured using a pole with a mark every six

28
00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:22.136
feet. "Twain" is an old word meaning "two," so when the sailor checking the depth of the water saw that it covered only two marks on the pole, he would yell out, "Mark twain!" as a

29
00:03:22.137 --> 00:03:28.302
warning. From the familiar cry of the steamboats on the Mississippi, the writer Mark Twain was

30
00:03:28.303 --> 00:03:32.917
born. Mark Twain wrote numerous novels and short stories during his

31
00:03:32.918 --> 00:03:40.505
career. But the two works that gained him the most fame were The Adventures of Tom Sawyer and Adventures of Huckleberry Finn

32
00:03:40.506 --> 00:03:49.221
. These novels were his masterpieces, but of the two, Huckleberry Finn is recognized as the more exceptional piece of

33
00:03:49.222 --> 00:03:58.158
literature. It is considered one of the first great American novels because it addresses the social and moral norms of the day like no other work before

34
00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:09.169
it. Tom Sawyer was published in 1876, and unlike Huckleberry Finn , it carries no clear social message, but simply follows the exploits of

35
00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:14.430
two boys. However, Tom Sawyer has been, and continues to be, popular among

36
00:04:14.431 --> 00:04:19.760
youthful readers. The main characters of both books are said to be based on Twain’s own

37
00:04:19.761 --> 00:04:29.913
childhood friends. Adventures of Huckleberry Finn , published in 1884, also draws upon Mark Twain’s own life, including his experience

38
00:04:29.914 --> 00:04:34.745
of Southern slavery. In truth, Mark Twain’s father owned one slave and his

39
00:04:34.746 --> 00:04:44.230
uncle owned several. As a child, Mark Twain spent countless summers playing in the slaves’ houses, listening to their stories, and singing and celebrating

40
00:04:44.231 --> 00:04:54.623
with them. Huckleberry Finn is narrated by the title character named Huck, a thirteen-year-old boy who journeys down the Mississippi River with his friend Jim, a

41
00:04:54.624 --> 00:04:57.770
runaway slave. This novel is a story of discovery

42
00:04:57.771 --> 00:05:05.499
and friendship. White characters are depicted as foolish or cruel in comparison to the black main character, who is wise

43
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.774
and unselfish. It was controversial in its time and continues to be

44
00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:13.596
so today. Mark Twain also toured the world as a reporter

45
00:05:13.597 --> 00:05:19.379
and lecturer. He traveled to Hawaii as a reporter for The Sacramento Union, and then to Italy

46
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:25.425
and France. Throughout his travels, he published works making fun of American and European prejudices

47
00:05:25.426 --> 00:05:27.989
and manners. His fame and fortune continued

48
00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:36.754
to grow. In 1870, he was financially secure enough to marry his sweetheart, Olivia Langdon, whom he had been courting for

49
00:05:36.755 --> 00:05:40.846
two years. The couple had four children together but lost three

50
00:05:40.847 --> 00:05:46.861
to illness. In 1897, a false obituary was written about Twain declaring

51
00:05:46.862 --> 00:05:54.059
him dead. He responded in his typically humorous fashion by stating that "the reports of my death have been greatly

52
00:05:54.060 --> 00:06:00.787
exaggerated." During this period of his life, Twain made a number of bad investment decisions and lost most of his

53
00:06:00.788 --> 00:06:06.150
money. In order to restore his wealth, he frequently traveled around the world on lecture

54
00:06:06.151 --> 00:06:12.164
tours. In 1904, while the couple was living in Italy, Twain’s wife became ill and passed

55
00:06:12.165 --> 00:06:15.454
away. After her death, Twain returned to the United

56
00:06:15.455 --> 00:06:19.816
States. In his later years, Mark Twain’s works became darker in

57
00:06:19.817 --> 00:06:25.989
tone. The deaths of three of his children and of his wife had an indelible impact on his life and

58
00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:32.870
writing. His writing, shaped as always by his travels and experiences, now focused on human greed and

59
00:06:32.871 --> 00:06:37.986
cruelty. It grew increasingly critical of government, and some people even labeled him a

60
00:06:37.987 --> 00:06:46.694
traitor. Not all of his work was published during his lifetime because some magazines and newspapers were afraid doing so would damage their

61
00:06:46.695 --> 00:06:55.458
reputations. But whether his words were light and humorous or dark and critical, Mark Twain never stopped observing and writing about all the things around him.

62
00:06:55.459 --> 00:07:02.734
He wrote of both romance and reality, and described both the positives and negatives of a quickly changing world.

63
00:07:02.735 --> 00:07:08.557
In 1909, Twain wrote, "I came in with Halley’s Comet . . . and I expect to go out with it."

64
00:07:08.558 --> 00:07:09.946
And so he did.

65
00:07:09.947 --> 00:07:17.323
He died at the age of 74 on April 21, 1910, one day after the comet had passed.

